WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:06.150
Hollywood runs on hype, from a project's earliest
conception to its world premiere and beyond,

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:10.080
the entire movie industry depends on getting
audiences as pumped as possible for their

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.630
blockbuster product.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:15.910
In fact, there's an entire industry within
the industry, filled with people whose jobs

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:17.950
depend on getting the word out.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.690
Unfortunately, the folks whose job it is to
market and hype up a film aren't always on

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:25.640
the same page as the people tasked with actually
making a movie.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:29.140
Announcements are made, interviews are given,
and all of a sudden, the film just up and

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:31.250
disappears from the studio's release schedule.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:35.860
It can happen for all sorts of surprising
reasons, but regardless, all we're left with

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:39.790
is the disappointment of knowing we'll never
get to see the movie we were promised.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:44.940
Here's a look at some overhyped films that
quietly disappeared from the release schedule.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.100
Dark Universe

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.171
The Dark Universe will probably go down in
Hollywood history as a prime example of the

00:00:50.171 --> 00:00:54.270
high risk a studio runs of being embarrassed
by overhyped films.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:58.450
In an attempt to mirror the Marvel Cinematic
Universe's interwoven narratives and massive

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:03.420
box office success, Universal Studios looked
to their own crossover-worthy characters,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.929
classic monsters like Dracula, the Wolfman,
and more.

00:01:06.929 --> 00:01:10.619
While it wouldn't be the first time Universal
had made crossover movies, the ideas behind

00:01:10.619 --> 00:01:14.499
this new franchise seemed to borrow more from
superhero and action movies than the gothic

00:01:14.499 --> 00:01:17.689
influences of their early 1900s forebears.

00:01:17.689 --> 00:01:21.659
Universal even invested in a special "Dark
Universe" logo to introduce films that would

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:23.799
take place in this universe.

00:01:23.799 --> 00:01:24.799
So...

00:01:24.799 --> 00:01:26.079
"What happened?"

00:01:26.079 --> 00:01:30.619
Unfortunately, despite all-star casting like
Tom Cruise in The Mummy and Johnny Depp as

00:01:30.619 --> 00:01:35.009
the the Invisible Man, the Dark Universe was
quickly derailed when The Mummy bombed at

00:01:35.009 --> 00:01:36.369
the box office.

00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:40.820
Bride of Frankenstein, which was rumored to
star Angelina Jolie in the title role, was

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:45.119
quickly taken off of Universal's schedule,
and, despite a massive launch announcement

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:49.119
with a Photoshopped lineup of the stars, the
creatives behind the would-be franchise moved

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:50.679
on to other projects.

00:01:50.679 --> 00:01:53.950
It looks like the real dark universe was ours,
all along.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:55.609
Divergent: Ascendant

00:01:55.609 --> 00:01:58.639
The Divergent adaptations seemed like a sure
thing.

00:01:58.639 --> 00:02:03.100
Based on the hit Young Adult novels by Veronica
Roth, the dystopian action series seemed like

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:06.760
the natural heir to the Hunger Games hysteria
that dominated the box office.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:11.380
Unfortunately, while the first two films managed
to earn a respectable return on investment,

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.800
they weren't quite as bankable as the studio
might've hoped.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:19.290
Even so, Lionsgate went forward with splitting
the final film into two, like Harry Potter,

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:23.600
Twilight, and The Hunger Games before it,
and that decision turned out to be a huge

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:24.600
mistake.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.549
Allegiant was only loosely based on the book
version of the same name, and not only were

00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:33.069
the reviews for the film scathing, but fans
failed to turn up at the ticket booth as well.

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:38.150
As a result, the would-be final film, Ascendant,
fell apart at the seams by losing its director

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:41.900
and cast, and then being shunted off to the
television circuit, where it's remained in

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.769
production limbo.

00:02:43.769 --> 00:02:45.549
World War Z 2

00:02:45.549 --> 00:02:50.099
Based on the gripping bestseller by Max Brooks,
World War Z came loaded with a passionate

00:02:50.099 --> 00:02:53.689
fan-base, including some of Hollywood's most
prestigious actors.

00:02:53.689 --> 00:02:58.370
After a bidding war against Leonardo DiCaprio's
production group Appian Way, Brad Pitt's company

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:03.510
Plan B optioned and produced the film adaptation
of the apocalyptic zombie thriller.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.850
Unfortunately, things start going downhill
from there as the film became an entirely

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:10.140
different kind of disaster movie.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:14.609
After the initial filming process was complete,
the flick went through a massive reshoot process

00:03:14.609 --> 00:03:19.220
that basically replaced the film's entire
third act and made it unrecognizable to readers

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:20.700
of Brooks' version.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.970
Although it still went on to score a solid
half-billion dollars at the box office, the

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:27.849
film's ballooned production costs ate into
those profits.

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:32.060
A sequel was still announced by Paramount
Pictures, due to the bounty of book material

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.530
left to bring to the big screen.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:39.420
"We have so many ideas and so much information
that we think we've got a lot of stuff to

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:40.569
mine from."

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:45.239
However, the film's release date has continued
to be bumped back again and again to the point

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:48.819
that it seems like director David Fincher
might never get a chance to resurrect this

00:03:48.819 --> 00:03:50.010
franchise.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:52.110
Terminator Genisys 2

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:55.200
The first Terminator: Genisys left a lot to
be desired.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.019
For instance, the movie was straight up incomplete
and basically relied on the eventual sequel

00:04:00.019 --> 00:04:01.489
to complete the narrative.

00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:05.790
Unfortunately, planning for a sequel can backfire
if the released movie underperforms, and that's

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:07.870
exactly what happened with Genisys.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:11.529
The film did have some serious star power
with Emilia Clarke and the return of Arnold

00:04:11.529 --> 00:04:13.760
Schwarzenegger, who made good on that promise
…

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.400
"I'll be back."

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.340
(Please Arnold, don't.)

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:20.310
However, the movie underperformed in the US
and fell short of its production budget by

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.280
about $60 million.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:26.080
What was meant to be the birth of a new trilogy
turned out to be a non-starter, and the second

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:31.160
installment was removed from Paramount's upcoming
releases, paving the way for yet another adjustment

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:35.920
of franchise continuity with a sequel that
would ignore everything after Judgement Day.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:37.449
Fantastic Four 2

00:04:37.449 --> 00:04:42.220
Years before anyone seriously believed Marvel
Comics could spin off anything approaching

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:46.520
a cinematic universe, the venerable publisher
fell on hard financial times and ended up

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:49.880
selling off the film rights to some of their
most popular characters.

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:54.940
Spider-Man landed at Sony and the Fantastic
Four and X-Men landed at Fox, and without

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:59.770
access to those heroes, the fledgling Marvel
Studios turned to characters like Iron Man,

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:04.460
Captain America, and Thor, which were at the
time properties that had never achieved quite

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:05.789
as much mainstream popularity.

00:05:05.789 --> 00:05:10.650
Of course, this all ended up working out pretty
well for Marvel, and for Fox and Sony, both

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:15.509
of whom found themselves sitting on adaptation
options that had never been more valuable.

00:05:15.509 --> 00:05:20.210
As a result, Spidey and the Fantastic Four
have both spawned multiple franchises, but

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:24.440
while Sony has managed to hit box office paydirt
with its Spider-Man movies on more than one

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:28.120
occasion, the Fantastic Four films have had
a rougher go of it.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:32.410
Fox's first pass at the franchise made money,
but the reviews were less than kind, prompting

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:37.819
a reboot in 2015, and that attempt fared even
worse than its predecessors, earning godawful

00:05:37.819 --> 00:05:41.830
reviews and the worst grosses of any theatrical
release in the series.

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:46.940
Still, there's no benefit to holding film
rights you can't use, so Fox executives consistently

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:51.400
reiterated that a sequel was definitely happening,
regardless of what happened with the first

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:52.400
film.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.780
Considering that the latest release date promised
a June 2017 arrival, we can pretty safely

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:59.350
assume that the sequel won't be happening.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:03.610
The Disney-Fox merger deal might mean that
Marvel's First Family will get another shot

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:08.870
at a movie release sometime, although it likely
won't be anytime soon, and it won't be a follow-up

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:13.509
to any past Fantastic Four film...which is
probably just as well.

00:06:13.509 --> 00:06:14.580
Sinister Six

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:19.220
Sony didn't just own the rights to Spider-Man;
the studio also laid claim to an assortment

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:23.360
of other characters related to everyone's
favorite friendly neighborhood web-slinger.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:27.650
In order to compete with Marvel's growing
domination of the superhero genre, Sony announced

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:32.360
plans in 2014 to make a Sinister Six movie,
to be directed by Drew Goddard.

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:36.880
The film's conceit was fairly ambitious, bringing
together six of Spidey's most famous villains

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:39.720
together as the titular supervillain team.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:43.650
Promotional materials were even created to
build fan fervor following the release of

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:45.530
The Amazing Spider-Man 2.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:50.490
Sadly, while everyone involved seemed invested
in making Sinister Six a reality, the Amazing

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:54.650
Spider-sequel underperformed, paving the way
for a deal between Marvel and Sony that was

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:59.620
struck just in time to reboot the character
again with Tom Holland in the role, and have

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:04.780
Spidey make his Marvel Cinematic Universe
debut alongside Captain America and Iron Man

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:06.729
in Captain America: Civil War.

00:07:06.729 --> 00:07:10.970
It led to all sorts of Spider-Man goodness,
but the collateral damage of the deal included

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:12.669
Goddard's Sinister Six.

00:07:12.669 --> 00:07:16.770
Sony hasn't given up on Spider-spinoffs, and
although subsequent plans to continue building

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:20.710
out the character's shared universe have hit
some snags of their own, fans can still look

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:26.060
forward to a big-screen solo outing for at
least one of Spider-Man's most popular foes.

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:27.060
Uncharted

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:31.310
When it comes to experiences that feel like
watching an Indiana Jones movie, you're mostly

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:36.580
limited to getting a doctorate in archeology
or going on the Indiana Jones ride at Disneyland.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:40.940
Still, the Uncharted video game series manages
to scratch the particular itch of watching

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:44.909
a handsome and dashing adventurer run around
giant monuments while flirting with pretty

00:07:44.909 --> 00:07:48.020
girls and almost getting killed by amoral
thieves.

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:52.699
The series follows Nathan Drake as an artifact
hunter whose adventures in picturesque locations

00:07:52.699 --> 00:07:57.389
and grand action set pieces seemed absolutely
made for the big screen.

00:07:57.389 --> 00:08:01.500
While movie adaptations of video games tend
to be poorly received, adapting Uncharted

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.800
seemed like a perfect continuation of the
pulpy adventure movies that Indiana Jones

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:07.010
had traded in.

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:11.729
Unfortunately, the movie ran through a series
of difficult turnovers as director after director

00:08:11.729 --> 00:08:13.389
left the project.

00:08:13.389 --> 00:08:18.220
Joe Carnahan was tapped to write the script
in 2016, but the Uncharted movie was removed

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:21.750
from Sony's upcoming release list only a few
months later.

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:27.009
Still, fans don't need to lose hope completely;
a since-deleted Instagram post by Tom Holland

00:08:27.009 --> 00:08:30.060
seemed to imply that he would be starring
in the long-awaited movie.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:34.251
Much like any archaeologist, fans will just
have to wait and see if what comes out of

00:08:34.251 --> 00:08:36.180
the ground is treasure...or trash.

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:37.760
Thanks for watching!

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:40.230
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:41.901
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

